# TDVP K230 source-maintenance tools

`tdvp-source-tools` is an r10 installation profile, not a private copy of
programs or libraries. It brings in independently versioned source-maintenance
tools using exact dependencies:

* `tree`, `tdvp-less`, `file`, and `which` for layout, log inspection, type
  detection, and command discovery; the firmware's base `less` is not replaced;
* `tdvp-wget` for simple scripted HTTPS/FTP retrieval, using the reviewed
  OpenSSL, zlib, and CA-store closure without GnuTLS, PSL, IDN/IRI, or c-ares;
  the firmware's base `wget` is not replaced;
* `dos2unix`, GNU `tdvp-grep`, `sed`, `findutils`, and `gawk`/`tdvp-awk` for
  source-tree and text maintenance; the firmware's base `grep` and `awk` are
  not replaced;
* `jq` for JSON inspection and transformation.

The runtime libraries remain independently owned and are pulled transitively:
`jq -> libjq`, `tdvp-grep -> libpcre2-8`, and
`gawk -> libreadline -> libncursesw`. The profile carries only this document;
uninstalling it does not remove tools that are still required by another
installed package.

`curl` and `rsync` are deliberately not r10 feed dependencies. The K230 base
already owns `curl`, but the locked source build differs byte-for-byte and was
rejected by CI run `33901074012`; `rsync` remains paused while its `libpopt`
provider is target-owned. This profile neither replaces those base commands
nor claims that their candidate IPKs are installable.

`tmux` is also deliberately absent until TDVP has independently owned,
OpenSSL-capable libevent runtime packages. The matching firmware SDK enables
that optional branch globally, so accepting the current command leaf would
otherwise create an undeclared OpenSSL runtime dependency.

After installing the unsigned r10 candidate on a real K230 device, verify:

    tdvp-tree --version
    tdvp-less --version
    file --version
    tdvp-which grep
    tdvp-wget --version
    tdvp-wget --https-only -O /tmp/tdvp-wget-example.html https://example.com/
    printf '{"answer":42}\n' | jq .answer
    printf 'A\r\nB\r\n' > /tmp/tdvp-crlf.txt && tdvp-dos2unix /tmp/tdvp-crlf.txt
    printf 'alpha\nbeta\n' | tdvp-grep -P '^a'
    printf 'alpha\n' | tdvp-sed 's/a/A/'
    tdvp-find /tmp -maxdepth 1 -type f -print | tdvp-xargs -r -n1 basename
    tdvp-awk 'BEGIN { print 1 + 1 }'

Before this real-device install/run/uninstall/rollback test succeeds, r10
remains an unsigned candidate and must not be promoted to the public feed.
